# Shell scripts are LF in the working tree, always, on every platform.
#
# Git already STORED them as LF — that was never the problem. The problem is the
# checkout: on Windows, core.autocrlf rewrites them to CRLF on the way out, and
# bash then reads `set -o pipefail\r` and answers
#
#     set: pipefail
#     : invalid option name
#
# ...which failed ten tests in station/tests/test_gsu_update.py on every run of
# the suite from a Windows checkout. Nothing was wrong with the script, the test
# or the change being tested, so the failures became background noise — and ten
# permanently-red tests are how a real failure gets waved past.
#
# It is not only a dev-environment annoyance. These scripts are copied into the
# station image; an image built from a checkout with CRLF endings would ship a
# `gsu-update.sh` that cannot run at all, on hardware in the field, where the
# thing it cannot run is the updater. Releases are built on Linux today, so this
# has never bitten — "today" being the operative word.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same argument, same blast radius: these are read by a shell or by systemd on
# the station, and neither tolerates a stray carriage return.
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
